 ### 1.3 Evidence behind the design
 
-The local research distinguishes useful reflective feedback from proof of an outcome. A completion judge can find semantic omissions, but objective tasks need observable state and independent assertions. This follows the findings in [completion-judge research](/Users/johansardar/Documents/Codex/personal-assistant-harness/research/v3/completion-judge.md), [self-repair research](/Users/johansardar/Documents/Codex/personal-assistant-harness/research/v3/self-repair.md), and [self-improvement harness research](/Users/johansardar/Documents/Codex/personal-assistant-harness/research/v3/selfimprove-harnesses.md).
-
-Research on LLM judges documents biases; research on intrinsic self-correction shows that reflection without external feedback can fail. These support measuring Toy’s reviewer and retaining external evidence, rather than assuming that a second opinion establishes correctness. [MT-Bench judge study](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05685), [self-correction study](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.01798).
-
-Agent evaluation should include the state left in the environment, rather than grading only the conversation. [τ-bench](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12045), [Anthropic agent evaluation guidance](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/demystifying-evals-for-ai-agents).
+The judge reads the conversation and stop decision. Most behavioral failures have no independent proof object. Existing receipts/readbacks still check deterministic claims such as a calendar write, durable wake, published digest or intercepted send. Missing proof does not prohibit useful continuation, and a model's claim does not substitute for a deterministic check that exists. P1/P5 govern verification of the harness itself through its largest reachable interface.
 
